  "interpretation": "<p>COMMENTS:</p>\n<p>Aeskulisa LKM 1 by IFA detects antibodies against liver-kidney microsomes in human serum. The assay is a tool for the diagnosis of autoimmune hepatitis.</p>\n<p>Autoimmune hepatitis (AIH) is a distinct chronic inflammatory liver disease. Hepatomegaly and spleenomegaly are the most common pathological findings associated with AIH. Abnormalities of the immune system that mark AIH include autoantibody to liver antigens, hyper-gammaglobulinemia, and an increased CD4/CD8 ratio in peripheral blood and liver. Liver-Kidney Microsomal (LKM1) antibodies can be induced not only by autoimmune mechanisms, but also by drugs such as tannic acid, dihydralazine, halothane, phenytoin, phenobarbital, carbamazepine and by Hepatitis C and D infections.</p>\n<p>1 This test alone should not be considered diagnostic. All other factors including the clinical history of the patients and other serological or biopsy results must also be take into account.</p>\n<p>2 Autoantibodies can be activated or induced by a wide range of drugs including oral contraceptives and antibodies.</p>",
